load @/code/type/term
  find term

# The identity type, the observational equality the kernel computes with. For two values of one type, the identity
# type is the type of proofs that they are equal. Reflexivity gives the canonical proof that a value equals itself, and
# the eliminator, the J rule, says a property that holds at reflexivity holds along any proof of equality, which is how
# every fact about equality is derived. On top of this intensional core the kernel makes equality observational: the
# identity at a function type reduces to pointwise identity, so two functions equal at every argument are equal, and
# function extensionality is the identity function rather than a postulate. The same computation gives the pair case
# componentwise, and the dependent pair case by transport. This is the observational discipline: full function and
# pair extensionality and quotients, while type-checking stays decidable, without the cost of cubical machinery.
# Univalence is deliberately out of scope; this is a programming language's logic, not a synthetic homotopy theory.
#
# The rules. Reflexivity: every value equals itself. Elimination (J): a motive true at reflexivity is true along any
# equality proof. Function extensionality, computed not postulated: the identity at a function type reduces to the
# pointwise identity. Transport: a proof that two values are equal carries any structure indexed by the first to the
# same structure indexed by the second, the dependent companion of the eliminator.
form identity-type
  link space, like term
  link left, like term
  link right, like term
